``` Should the `_g_` in the middle of `g_seeded_g_prng_zero` and the other one still be kept? Current behavior with a test that calls both:
```C++
SeedRandomStateForTest(SeedRand::ZEROS);
SeedRandomStateForTest(SeedRand::FIXED_SEED);
```
Resulting in `seeded_g_prng_zero` not being set... which I guess is a way of detecting that something is wrong.

My suggestion would make `seeded_g_prng_zero` _retain_ the fact that `ZEROS` had been used.

None of the above solutions detect this invalid case:
```C++
SeedRandomStateForTest(SeedRand::FIXED_SEED);
...fuzz target code using randomness...
SeedRandomStateForTest(SeedRand::ZEROS);
```

It would be more robust with my suggested change, + adding a separate `seeded_g_prng_fixed` bool which is (`|=`-)set when `FIXED_SEED` is used, so that we could error on that. Is there a (micro-)benchmark that would improve?

Also, this would make it harder to use in other tests, such as the unit tests. Yes, because this is only about the global prng, not about any local ones. > adding a separate `seeded_g_prng_fixed` bool

No strong opinion. There is only one call to `SeedRandomForTest(SeedRand::FIXED_SEED)`, so hopefully it won't be a problem going forward and the code doesn't have to be written and reviewed. Happy to reconsider, if other reviewers want it as well. It seems more important to make the code reproducible and stable before extended fuzzing. Also, if the crash is reproducible, it will be re-detected anyway when the code is fixed. So I think the extra code isn't needed. thx. Will fix if I have to re-push. In the future, it would be better to check this in the CI, see https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/31308 (Would prefer `g_seeded_global_prng_zero` in that case, even though I admit it is verbose). This is about excluding test-only code from production (currently fuzz-only since *check_globals.h/cpp* is in *fuzz/util/*). Not sure why that would be undesirable. Even though the conditional adds verbosity, it adds context and decreases need for comments.

