```C++
BOOST_AUTO_TEST_CASE(decode_malformed_test) {
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%%xhello th+ere \xff"), "%%xhello th+ere \xff");

    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%"), "%");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%%"), "%%");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%%%"), "%%%");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%%%%"), "%%%%");

    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("+"), "+");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("++"), "++");

    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("?"), "?");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("??"), "??");

    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%G1"), "%G1");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%2"), "%2");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%ZX"), "%ZX");

    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("valid%20string%G1"), "valid string%G1");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%20invalid%ZX"), " invalid%ZX");
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(urlDecode("%20%G1%ZX"), " %G1%ZX");
}
```

Since the original implementation seems to include a case for handling `?` and `+` as well, I've added tests to make sure they're kept. `ptr` seems unused, we might as well inline it to:
```C++
std::from_chars(&url_encoded[i + 1], &url_encoded[i + 3], decoded_value, 16).ec == std::errc{})
```
(note, I've used `std::errc{}` instead, seems we're using that more often in the codebase) we could unify these two `res += c` cases by merging the two conditions:
```C++
std::string UrlDecode(const std::string& url_encoded) {
    std::string res;
    res.reserve(url_encoded.size());

    for (size_t i = 0; i < url_encoded.size(); ++i) {
        int decoded_value = 0;
        if (url_encoded[i] == '%' &&
            i + 2 < url_encoded.size() &&
            std::from_chars(&url_encoded[i + 1], &url_encoded[i + 3], decoded_value, 16).ec == std::errc{}) {
            res += static_cast<char>(decoded_value);
            i += 2;
        } else {
            res += url_encoded[i];
        }
    }

    return res;
}
``` is this passing for the old implementation?
