My understanding is that `GlobalMutex` should only be used for mutexes that are defined as global variables.
```suggestion
    mutable Mutex m_mutex;
``` If `nullptr` is not allowed, then might as well pass const reference. Also, can erase it right away, without lookup first.

```suggestion
    void RemoveConnectionInternal(const evhttp_connection& conn) EXCLUSIVE_LOCKS_REQUIRED(m_mutex)
    { 
        m_tracker.erase(&conn);
        if (m_tracker.empty()) { 
            m_cv.notify_all();
        } 
    } 
``` If an entry is not in the map, then `operator[]` would insert it with a default value (0 in this case). So the following is equivalent but shorter:
```suggestion
        ++m_tracker[conn];
``` whitespace:
```suggestion
        if (it != m_tracker.end() && it->second > 0) {
``` to avoid grabbing the mutex twice in a row in the request tracker, could call `.CountActiveConnections` only once:
```
        if (auto connections = g_requests.CountActiveConnections(); connections != 0) {
            LogPrint(BCLog::HTTP, "Waiting for %d connections to stop HTTP server\n", connections);
        }
``` Calling `CountActiveConnections()` just once will also avoid a weird message like "Waiting for 0 connections to stop HTTP server" which can happen if the function is called two times and the first call returns >0 and the second call returns 0 (the connection was closed in the meantime, after the first and before the second call). Ah right, didn't think about that. Thanks, fixed. Thanks, fixed (+ in line below) Outdated since incorporating [this suggestion](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/28551#issuecomment-1743499318) to use an iterator. Makes sense - considered it but didn't think about `CountActiveConnections()` acquiring a lock. Definitely an improvement, so incorporated this suggestion, thanks. Beautiful, wasn't aware of that `operator[]` behaviour. Thanks, adopted (putting it inside `WITH_LOCK`) You need this:

